# Narrowing the Docker build context to services/portal-api/ (see
# .github/workflows/push.yml, release.yml, cron.yml, ci.yml -- the context
# MUST be this directory so `COPY requirements.txt .` in the Dockerfile
# resolves to this file, not the stale root requirements.txt) means a bare
# `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile now reaches everything in this directory,
# including local-dev-only files that were never meant to leave a
# developer's machine. In particular .envrc ships a default DB_PASS value
# and dev-mode Flask env vars -- baking it into the runtime image is
# exactly the kind of thing this file exists to prevent.

# direnv local dev config (contains a default DB_PASS)
.envrc
.env
.env.*

# Schema/migration authority -- backend-database.md: migrations run
# manually or via a separate K8s Job, never from app startup, so the
# runtime image (this Dockerfile's CMD execs hypercorn directly against
# app:create_app()) has no code path that touches these at all.
alembic/
alembic.ini

# Local/dev-only entry point -- run.py's own docstring: "the local/dev
# equivalent" of the container's CMD, which invokes hypercorn directly and
# never imports this file.
run.py
scripts/

# Compiled-from source; only requirements.txt (hash-pinned, --require-hashes)
# is installed.
requirements.in

# VCS/tooling noise
.git/
.gitignore
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
